<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hamar is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mjosa">Lake Mjøsa</a>, the administrative centre of Innlandet county. It became the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1152 and grew around its cathedral, whose ruins are now protected under a glass shelter. The medieval town declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and a new town was founded on the site in 1849.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamar.jpg" alt="Hamar" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bjørn Christian Tørrissen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamar-Aerial-2022.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
